From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:40:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tccgen: constant propagation through local scalars

A local variable (integer or pointer, not volatile, address never taken)
whose last store was a constant is known to hold it: a read of the
variable is the constant, so that a condition on it folds like a literal
constant and the dead branch does not reference what it calls.  The Linux
kernel relies on this in an optimizing compiler: an IS_ENABLED() value
that passes through a local (dma_direct_alloc()'s set_uncached,
WARN_ON_ONCE()'s __ret_warn_once) or through the return value of a stub
(swiotlb_find_pool(), shmem_allowable_huge_orders(), acpi_device_handle()
without their subsystems) guards a call to a function that does not exist
in the configuration; tcc kept the call and the link failed, which four
kernel patches of the tinycc-os-kernel project worked around (a fifth,
shmem's, stays: another caller passes the constant through a function
parameter).

Stores are always generated; only reads are folded.  The knowledge (Sym
kst/kval) follows the parse: a store of a plain constant (not the address
of a symbol, whose Sym may be gone when the variable is read) makes the
variable known, any other store or an asm output makes it unknown, and
the control flow is treated conservatively: where two paths join only
what both agree on stays known (KSnap snapshots of if/else, ?:, && and
||, kacc accumulators of the paths that break or continue to a point, and
of the gotos to a label), the variables a loop may assign are unknown
throughout the loop (its tokens are scanned at the head), a label that a
later goto or a computed goto can reach makes everything unknown (the
gotos of a function are scanned before its body is compiled), and dead
code records nothing.  __builtin_constant_p() sees the known value too.

For the scan of the gotos every function body is now compiled from saved
tokens, like the static ones already were.  A #pragma inside such a body
(pack, comment) took effect when the body was saved, not where it stands:
pragma_parse() now keeps the directive in the saved tokens
(TOK_LINEFEED TOK_PRAGMA ... TOK_LINEFEED) and next1() executes it when
they are compiled (push_macro, pop_macro and once act at preprocessing
time, as before); a scan of saved tokens skips them.

Fix 119 of the tinycc-os-kernel project.
